
  [1mThe ESM decision has a consequence in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m I want to surface.[0m For a Node test to [38;5;153mimport[39m [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m, Node has to
  treat [38;5;153m.js[39m as ESM, which means [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m at the root. But that flag is package-wide, and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and
  [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m use CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m — the flag would break that program. The fix is one line: add a nested
  [38;5;153msrc/package.json[39m containing [38;5;153m{"type": "commonjs"}[39m, which pins the existing Node code to its current semantics and
  leaves both files untouched. Root goes ESM, [38;5;153msrc/[39m stays as it is. I flag it because "add a preferences module" quietly
  turning into "edit the unrelated Node program" is exactly the kind of collateral damage worth refusing.

  [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes[0m from [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m. Real consequence, worth
  repeating: after this, opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it stops working — module scripts need an [38;5;153mhttp://[39m origin.
  You'd run [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m in the repo and open [38;5;153mlocalhost:8000[39m.

  [1mTesting.[0m Node's built-in runner ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m, Node 18+) fits this repo's zero-dependency posture — no packages, no
  config, one [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m script. Tests inject a fake storage object into [38;5;153mcreatePreferences[39m, so they cover the
  round trip, the JSON edge cases ([38;5;153mundefined[39m, cycles, [38;5;153mNaN[39m), corrupt-data reads, the unavailable-at-startup fallback,
  and the mid-session quota failure with its overlay — all without a browser. I'd skip linters, e2e, and fuzzing here;
  adding ESLint and Prettier means the first dependencies in a four-file project, and that's your call to make later,
  not something to slip into this change.

  The open question is how far the wiring goes. Right now the app has no settings at all, so a storage module on its own
  has no consumer — it'd be tested but never exercised by the actual page, and you couldn't verify "persists across
  sessions" by reloading.

